{"page_content": "ACME/Flame Design Final Report (2023) \n \n6 April 2023 \n63 \n \n \n \nWhile Flame Design\u2019s primary objective is to determine the conditions under which flames are soot-free \nor soot producing, a secondary objective is to establish flammability limits for these spherical flames. We \nhave observed interesting phenomena where the flames sometimes self-extinguish after growing \noscillations. These oscillations are apparent in the above graph, which shows the chemiluminescence from \nexcited molecules that drop to a lower energy state by emitting a photon at a specific wavelength. CH* \nchemiluminescence is the source of most of the blue seen in hydrocarbon flames and OH* \nchemiluminescence is in the ultraviolet. These two measurements are made with Photomultipiler Tubes \n(PMTs), where each tube is equipped with a narrow-band filter corresponding to an excited molecule. \n \nIn the example shown in the graph, the flame\u2019s oscillations lasted about 30 seconds and occurred at \nroughly 1 Hz, but they slowed in frequency as they grew in strength. The oscillations are evident in the \nimaging, where the flame locally quenches\u2014creating a hole in the flame. This hole allows for increased \nmixing of the fuel and oxygen, which in turn results in a recovery of the flame. The size of the opening in \nthe flame (which normally separates the fuel and oxygen) grows with time until the entire flame \nextinguishes. \n \nInterestingly, the graph shows that the CH* measurement was relatively steady before the start of the \noscillations in comparison with the decaying OH* emission. This difference will be further examined to \nimprove our understanding of flame extinction.", "metadata": {"source": "PSI-10_Reports_FinalRpt_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf", "page": 63}}